            436.968,
            329.472
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {},
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/166/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>where <math>P_i \\in \\mathbf{S}_+^n</math>, <math>i = 0, 1, \\dots, m</math>, the problem is called a <i>quadratically constrained quadratic program</i> (QCQP). In a QCQP, we minimize a convex quadratic function over a feasible region that is the intersection of ellipsoids (when <math>P_i \\succ 0</math>).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              104.03999999999999,
              366.696
            ],
            [
              465.12,
              366.696
            ],
            [
              465.12,
              405.504
            ],
            [
              104.03999999999999,
              405.504
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            104.03999999999999,
            366.696,
            465.12,
            405.504
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {},
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/166/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Quadratic programs include linear programs as a special case, by taking <math>P = 0</math> in (4.34). Quadratically constrained quadratic programs include quadratic programs (and therefore also linear programs) as a special case, by taking <math>P_i = 0</math> in (4.35), for <math>i = 1, \\dots, m</math>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              104.03999999999999,
              404.712
            ],
            [
              463.896,
              404.712
            ],
            [
              463.896,
              452.23199999999997
            ],
            [
              104.03999999999999,
              452.23199999999997
            ]
